---
import Layout from '@/components/Layout.astro';
import { getGuide, guideJsonLd } from '@/lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('/guides/which-hash');
---

<Layout
  title={guide.title}
  description={guide.description}
  path={guide.path}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[guideJsonLd(guide)]}
  narrow
>
  <article class="prose-tool max-w-prose">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[rgb(var(--text))] sm:text-3xl">
      {guide.title}
    </h1>

    <p>
      A hash function turns any input — a word, a file, a whole disk image — into a fixed-length
      digest. The same input always produces the same digest, a one-character change produces a
      completely different one, and the process cannot be reversed. That is all a hash guarantees.
      Choosing between MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256 and SHA-512 comes down to what you are using the digest
      for: spotting accidental damage, proving integrity against an attacker, authenticating a
      message, or storing a password.
    </p>

    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Job</th>
            <th scope="col">Pick</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Detect accidental corruption</td>
            <td>CRC-32, or SHA-256 if you want one tool for everything</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Verify a download against a published checksum</td>
            <td>Whatever the publisher used — normally SHA-256</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Prove a file has not been tampered with</td>
            <td>SHA-256 or SHA-512</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Authenticate a message with a shared key</td>
            <td>HMAC-SHA-256</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Store a user's password</td>
            <td>Argon2id, then scrypt, bcrypt or PBKDF2 — never a plain hash</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Match a legacy header or ETag</td>
            <td>The algorithm the format mandates, often MD5</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>MD5 and SHA-1 are broken for security</h2>
    <p>
      Both are still fast and still fine for catching a corrupted download, but neither can protect
      you from an attacker. MD5 (128-bit) has had practical <em>collisions</em> — two different inputs
      with the same digest — since 2004, and chosen-prefix collisions since 2008, which is what let
      researchers forge a certificate authority signature. SHA-1 (160-bit) fell in 2017: Google and
      CWI produced two different PDFs with the same SHA-1 digest. Generating a collision does not let
      anyone recover a password, but it does let them substitute one file for another, which breaks
      signatures, certificates, and any "the digest matches, so it is the same file" check against an
      adversary.
    </p>
    <p>
      So: use MD5 or SHA-1 only where the format dictates it (an object-storage ETag, a legacy
      protocol) and where nobody benefits from a collision. Never use them to sign anything, and
      never use them for passwords.
    </p>

    <h2>SHA-2 is the current default</h2>
    <p>
      SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512 are the SHA-2 family. There are no practical attacks on SHA-2, and
      all three are available through the browser's Web Crypto API, so they cost you nothing in portability.
      SHA-256 is the right default; SHA-512 is a fine choice on 64-bit hardware and whenever you want
      a wider margin; SHA-384 shows up in TLS and in Subresource Integrity attributes. SHA-3 (Keccak)
      is a later standard built on a different construction and stands ready as a backup — pick it if
      your platform offers it and you want independence from SHA-2, but there is no need to migrate
      away from SHA-256 today.
    </p>

    <h2>Why MD5 is unsafe for passwords</h2>
    <p>
      A plain hash of a password is the same every time, so an attacker who gets your database can
      simply look the digest up in a precomputed <em>rainbow table</em> of common passwords and read
      the original in microseconds. Even a random password falls to brute force because SHA-256 and
      its relatives were designed to be fast — fast enough to try billions of candidates per second on
      a GPU. "Fast" is exactly what you do not want when the input is a password.
    </p>
    <p>
      Password storage therefore uses deliberately slow, keyed, memory-hard constructions:
      <strong>Argon2id</strong> is the modern recommendation, with <strong>scrypt</strong>,{' '}
      <strong>bcrypt</strong> and <strong>PBKDF2</strong> as widely deployed alternatives. They mix
      in a random <em>salt</em> so identical passwords produce different digests, and their cost
      parameters are tuned to make guessing expensive. The{' '}
      <a href="/tools/pbkdf2-key-derivation">PBKDF2 key derivation</a> tool applies that idea to derive
      keys and verify passwords on-device.
    </p>

    <h2>When you need HMAC, not a hash</h2>
    <p>
      A plain SHA-256 digest is public: anyone can recompute it from the input, so it proves integrity
      but says nothing about who produced the message. <strong>HMAC</strong> (hash-based message
      authentication code) fixes that by mixing a secret key into the hashing, giving you an
      authenticated digest that an attacker cannot forge without the key. Reach for HMAC-SHA-256
      whenever you share a secret and need to prove a message came from the other party: API
      authentication, webhook signatures, signed session tokens. The
      <a href="/tools/hmac">HMAC generator</a> tool computes these directly.
    </p>
    <p>
      One clean way to think of it: a hash answers "did the bytes change?" and HMAC answers "did the
      bytes change, and do I trust who changed them?"
    </p>

    <h2>Using the tools</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/hash">Text Hash Generator</a> computes MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 and
      SHA-512 in hex, Base64 or base64url; <a href="/tools/file-hash">File Hash</a> does the same for
      an uploaded file; and <a href="/tools/hash-identifier">Hash Identifier</a> guesses which
      algorithm an unknown digest belongs to. For integrity on the web, the
      <a href="/tools/sri-hash-generator">SRI hash generator</a> builds the integrity attributes you
      drop on a <code>script</code> or <code>link</code> tag.
    </p>

    <h2>More guides</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/base64-explained">Base64 explained</a> — encoding, not hashing, and when to
        use it.
      </li>
      <li><a href="/guides/cron-cheat-sheet">Cron syntax cheat sheet</a> — scheduling with cron.</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/guides/color-contrast-wcag">Colour contrast and WCAG</a> — accessible colour
        choices.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</Layout>